105	## Raw defaults
106	
107	Each key under `[bootstrap.macos.defaults]` is a preferences domain. Quote
108	domains containing dots. Values map to the matching `defaults write` type:
109	
110	| TOML value | written as         | example                |
111	| ---------- | ------------------ | ---------------------- |
112	| boolean    | `-bool true/false` | `autohide = true`      |
113	| integer    | `-int <n>`         | `tilesize = 48`        |
114	| float      | `-float <n>`       | `scale = 1.5`          |
115	| string     | `-string <s>`      | `orientation = "left"` |
116	
117	Other plist shapes (arrays, dicts, dates, data) are not supported; entries
118	using them parse fine but are skipped with a warning, so configs written for
119	newer mise versions still work.
120	
121	## Semantics
122	
123	`[bootstrap.macos.defaults]` follows the same rules as
124	[`[bootstrap.packages]`](/bootstrap/packages/):
125	
126	- **Declarative and additive** — (domain, key) pairs merge across the
127	  [config hierarchy](/configuration.html) (global → project) as a union; a
128	  more local config overrides the value of a pair the global config declared
129	  but cannot remove it. mise never deletes a default.
130	- **OS-filtered** — on anything other than macOS the section is inert:
131	  `mise bootstrap macos defaults status` and `mise doctor` list the entries
132	  as skipped (so nothing is silently invisible) and
133	  `mise bootstrap macos defaults apply` ignores them, so a shared config
134	  authored for both Linux and macOS works as is.
135	- **Manual application only** — mise never writes defaults implicitly; only
136	  `mise bootstrap macos defaults apply` or the full `mise bootstrap` does, after the usual confirmation
137	  prompt.
